From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: nss: route the REO hash buckets to SW1

In NSS offload mode ath11k_hw_ipq5018_reo_setup() leaves the four
HAL_REO1_DEST_RING_CTRL_IX registers alone, so the hash buckets keep
their reset values (0x66543210, 0x66666666 on the QCN6122): some of
them point at ring 6, the REO2FW ring, which nothing drains in NSS
mode. Each peer gets a default route to REO2SW1 with hashing disabled
(ath11k_dp_peer_setup()), so the buckets only matter for frames REO
cannot tie to a peer route, around association and disassociation.
Those frames land on REO2FW (ring 19) and keep their RX buffers: the
ring fills up over hours and the 5 GHz RX path stalls (issue #53 on
the 256 MB AX53 v2; ring 19 head at 1008, tail at 0).

Point every bucket at REO2SW1 in NSS mode, where the peer routes go
anyway. Found and first fixed by tribhoon (PR #60); this is the
bucket change on its own, without the fragment ring part, so that the
effect of each can be told apart.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/hw.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/hw.c
@@ -1089,9 +1089,27 @@ static void ath11k_hw_ipq5018_reo_setup(
 	ath11k_hif_write32(ab, reo_base + HAL_REO1_AGING_THRESH_IX_3(ab),
 			   HAL_DEFAULT_REO_TIMEOUT_USEC);
 
-	/* REO Dest ring setup is not required in NSS offload case */
-	if (ab->nss.enabled)
+	/* In NSS offload mode every peer is routed to REO2SW1 with hashing
+	 * off, so the buckets only see frames without a peer route. Left at
+	 * their reset values some of them point at REO2FW, which nothing
+	 * drains here: send them all to REO2SW1 too.
+	 */
+	if (ab->nss.enabled) {
+		u32 sw1_map = HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 0 |
+			      HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 4 |
+			      HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 8 |
+			      HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 12 |
+			      HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 16 |
+			      HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 20 |
+			      HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 24 |
+			      HAL_HASH_ROUTING_RING_SW1 << 28;
+
+		ath11k_hif_write32(ab, reo_base + HAL_REO1_DEST_RING_CTRL_IX_0, sw1_map);
+		ath11k_hif_write32(ab, reo_base + HAL_REO1_DEST_RING_CTRL_IX_1, sw1_map);
+		ath11k_hif_write32(ab, reo_base + HAL_REO1_DEST_RING_CTRL_IX_2, sw1_map);
+		ath11k_hif_write32(ab, reo_base + HAL_REO1_DEST_RING_CTRL_IX_3, sw1_map);
 		return;
+	}
 
 	ath11k_hif_write32(ab, reo_base + HAL_REO1_DEST_RING_CTRL_IX_0,
 			   ring_hash_map);
